Pagination can mix a new search request with the previous search’s offset, and it can stop before reaching all results when a page adds no visible rows.
- {"id": "PR14-1", "severity": "high", "path": "Sources/EverythingApp/EverythingApp.swift:186", "evidence": "run() changes generation and request but retains the previous pages until the first response arrives. If an old near-end row appears during that interval, rowAppeared() sends the new query using the old pages.nextOffset and tags it with the new generation. The serial queue then processes that page after the new first page, and its response passes the generation check. If the old offset was 2,500, the new results skip rows 500\u20132,499; if the first request failed, the later page can populate results beneath an error footer.", "fix": "Invalidate or clear the old pages immediately when starting a search, and permit pagination only after that generation\u2019s first page has been accepted. Also associate each page response with the expected offset before appending."}
- {"id": "PR14-2", "severity": "medium", "path": "Sources/EverythingApp/EverythingApp.swift:240", "evidence": "A failed page is silently discarded and loadingMore is reset, but no retry is scheduled. If the near-end cells remain visible, their onAppear callbacks need not fire again, so pagination remains stuck and the footer gives no indication of the failure.", "fix": "Expose the page error and provide a reliable retry mechanism, or schedule a bounded retry while the current generation still needs that offset."}
- {"id": "PR14-3", "severity": "medium", "path": "Sources/EverythingCore/ResultPages.swift:48", "evidence": "A nonempty page may consist entirely of paths already loaded. For example, after 2,500 rows are loaded, at least 2,000 insertions above the boundary can make the next 2,000 daemon rows all repeats. The offset advances and hasMore remains true, but no new table cells appear to trigger another request, leaving later matches unreachable.", "fix": "After appending a page that adds no unique rows, continue fetching while hasMore remains true, with safeguards for cancellation and repeated failures."}
